            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "What does `:nmap <C-u>` and `:nmap <C-d>` show? Judging by behavior it is something like `<C-u>zvzz`.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, limited centering after movement command is one of a downsides of asynchronous animation. With that keybinding it will tell Neovim to start animate scroll but then immediately tell to forget previous animation and start animating `zvzz`.\r\n\r\nOne possible way to overcome this is to use [`MiniAnimate.execute_after()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-animate.txt#L443) as shown in example. For `<C-u>`/`<C-d>` it would be something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n  vim.keymap.set(\r\n    'n',\r\n    '<C-d>',\r\n    [[<Cmd>lua vim.cmd('normal! <C-d>'); MiniAnimate.execute_after('scroll', 'normal! zvzz')<CR>]]\r\n  )\r\n  vim.keymap.set(\r\n    'n',\r\n    '<C-u>',\r\n    [[<Cmd>lua vim.cmd('normal! <C-u>'); MiniAnimate.execute_after('scroll', 'normal! zvzz')<CR>]]\r\n  )\r\n```\r\n\r\n**But** it would often lead to jittering animations.",
